Massive improvement:
- desktop: 28.65ms → 7.33ms render (3.9x faster)
- mobile: 16.41ms → 2.87ms render (5.7x faster)

Now ~121fps desktop, ~267fps mobile equivalent (capped at 60 by rAF). Simulation 0.9ms. Plenty of headroom.

Now the remaining real issue: **evolutions still 0** in the run. Let me figure out why.

Looking at the last run: at level 15, weapons were spike3, trap1, nova1, orbit1, swarm1 and passives focus5, haste1, magnet1, might1.

So `spike` was only level 3, but `focus` reached level 5! The evolution needs spike 6 + focus 3. The bot's pick logic:
```js
const lvUpMain = els.findIndex(e => txt(...).includes(CFG.WEAPONS[main].name.toLowerCase()));
```
`main = owned[0]` = 'spike', name = 'Иглы'. The offer name for a weapon level-up is `'Иглы ур.4'` — lowercase 'иглы'. `txt` lowercases. Should match...

Hmm but the bot DID pick spike up to 3 and then stopped. And 46 picks were made for 15 levels?! That's suspicious — 46 picks vs 14 level-ups. Chests give 1 offer each. So ~32 chests?? At level 15 with 3 bosses... no, bosses=0.

Wait, "46 picks made" counts every click on an offer. But the loop clicks whenever `st.state === 'levelup'`. If the state stays 'levelup' after clicking (because another offer is queued), it clicks again. That's correct behavior.

But 46 picks for 14 levels means 32 extra offers from chests. Chests come from elites (every 60s after 2:30 — run was 198s = 3:18, so ~1 elite) and bosses (0). So maybe 1 chest. Not 32.

So something is re-showing the level-up screen. Let me look at `Game.chooseOffer`:

```js
Game.offers = [];
Game.state = Game.offerQueue > 0 ? 'playing' : 'playing';
if (Game.offerQueue > 0) {
  Game.offers = buildOffers();
  Game.state = 'levelup';
  Game.offerQueue--;
  emit('levelUpOffer', Game.offers);
}
```

OK. And in `addXp`:
```js
while (Game.xp >= Game.xpNeed) { ... Game.offerQueue++; ... }
if (Game.offerQueue > 0 && Game.state === 'playing') { ... Game.state='levelup'; Game.offerQueue--; }
```

Hmm. So per level, one offer. That's 14. Plus chests.

Unless... `Game.chooseOffer` is called but the state doesn't change because the click handler fires on a stale index. Actually the bot re-queries the DOM each time, so fine.

OH WAIT. I see it. In the bot loop:
```js
if (st.state === 'levelup') {
  const n = await page.locator('#offers .offer').count();
  if (n > 0) { ...click... }
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 60));
  continue;
}
```
It clicks, waits 60ms, then re-reads telemetry. But telemetry is only updated every 1000ms in main.js! So `st.state` is stale for up to a second. The bot may click repeatedly on... no, after the first click the offers are cleared (`box.innerHTML=''` happens in renderOffers, but if state changed to 'playing', renderOffers isn't called, so the OLD offers stay in the DOM!).

That's it. When you pick the last offer, `Game.offers = []` and state → 'playing', but `renderOffers` is never called to clear `#offers`. The screen switches away (show/hide via `hideAll()`? No!). Let me check: `Game.chooseOffer` doesn't call any UI function to hide the level-up screen. `renderOffers` shows it. So after choosing, the `#scrLevel` screen stays visible!

That's a REAL BUG: after picking an upgrade, the level-up overlay never hides. The player would be stuck looking at the offer screen... except the bot kept clicking the stale offers, which did nothing (Game.offers is empty, `Game.offers[index]` is undefined, `chooseOffer` returns early).

So the game is BROKEN: after the first level-up, the level-up screen never goes away. That explains 46 "picks" (repeated no-op clicks) and why the player never leveled their weapons properly — only the first offer of each level was applied... actually no, `Game.chooseOffer(i)` with empty offers returns early, so no effect. But the screen never hid, so the player is stuck!

This is the most important bug yet. Let me fix it: `Game.chooseOffer` must tell the UI to close the level-up screen.

Let me add an event `offerChosen` that the UI handles by calling `hideAll()`.

Let me fix in engine.js:
```js
Game.chooseOffer = function (index) {
  const o = Game.offers[index];
  if (!o) return;
  ...
  Game.offers = [];
  emit('offerChosen');   // UI closes the panel
  if (Game.offerQueue > 0) { ... show next ... }
};
```

And in ui.js add:
```js
else if (name === 'offerChosen') { if (Game.offerQueue === 0) hideAll(); }
```
But ordering: emit('offerChosen') then if offerQueue>0 renderOffers shows it again. Since emit happens before, and then renderOffers calls show('scrLevel'), that works. But to be safe, let me handle it: emit offerChosen AFTER deciding. Let me restructure:

```js
Game.offers = [];
if (Game.offerQueue > 0) {
  Game.offers = buildOffers();
  Game.state = 'levelup';
  Game.offerQueue--;
  emit('levelUpOffer', Game.offers);
} else {
  Game.state = 'playing';
  emit('offerChosen');
}
```

And UI: `else if (name === 'offerChosen') hideAll();`

That's clean.

Let me also make sure the initial `Game.state = Game.offerQueue > 0 ? 'playing' : 'playing';` weirdness is removed.

Let me apply.

